# component: scroll-area
# decisions: A11Y-22, A11Y-6, DOC-1, DOC-2, FOC-1, FOC-6
# hunks:
#   FOC-1, FOC-6: `outline-none focus-visible:ring-[3px] focus-visible:ring-ring/50
#     focus-visible:outline-1` goes from the viewport. The viewport IS a tab stop (see A11Y-6),
#     so `outline-none` would leave the one focusable part of this component with no affordance.
#   A11Y-6: the tab-stop half needs NO HUNK — Base UI's `ScrollArea.Viewport` sets
#     `tabIndex: hiddenState.x && hiddenState.y ? -1 : 0`, which is exactly 'a scroll viewport is
#     a tab stop only when it can actually scroll'. `scroll-area.test.tsx` asserts both
#     directions, so the day Base UI makes the viewport unconditionally tabbable the test says so.
#     The naming half IS a hunk (MK, D2 of the 2026-09-24 facelift plan): `aria-label`,
#     `aria-labelledby` and `tabIndex` are taken off the root and forwarded to the viewport — the
#     one focusable part — and a named viewport gets `role="region"`. Upstream spread them onto
#     the non-focusable root, so a keyboard user landed on an unnamed scroller.
#   A11Y-22: `viewportRef` is forwarded to `ScrollAreaPrimitive.Viewport`, so a consumer can
#     scroll it programmatically (`viewportRef.current.scrollTop = …`).
#   cn: `import { cn } from "cn"` becomes `@vegastack/design` — our npm layer owns `cn` (DOC-2).
#   formatting: the repository formatter (prettier defaults: semicolons, trailing commas)
#               reflows the file, and `registry-stamp.mjs` writes the provenance header the
#               three-copy model needs (DOC-1); those hunks carry no decision of their own.
